CAT = "shrmInterpersonal"; SEG = "INT"
# Interpersonal cluster (13.5% of the exam): Relationship Management, Communication.
Q = [
("Both parties give up part of what they want to reach a quick middle ground. This conflict style is:",
 "Compromising", ["Collaborating", "Accommodating", "Avoiding"],
 "Compromise splits the difference so each side gets part of its goal. Collaboration looks for a solution that fully meets both sides' needs."),
("Two managers work together until they find a solution that fully meets both their needs. This conflict style is:",
 "Collaborating", ["Compromising", "Competing", "Accommodating"],
 "Collaborating aims for a win-win that satisfies both parties, usually at the cost of more time. Compromising leaves each side partly satisfied."),
("Which conflict style fits best when the issue is trivial and tempers are running high?",
 "Avoiding", ["Competing", "Collaborating", "Compromising"],
 "Avoiding, or postponing, suits minor issues or moments when people need to cool down. Collaborating on a trivial issue costs more time than it is worth."),
("Negotiation that focuses on each side's underlying needs rather than fixed demands is:",
 "Interest-based bargaining", ["Position-based bargaining", "Distributive bargaining", "Concession bargaining"],
 "Interest-based (principled) bargaining explores why each side wants something to find options that serve both. Positional bargaining trades fixed demands."),
("In negotiation, the course a party will take if no agreement is reached is its:",
 "BATNA", ["Opening offer", "Anchor", "ZOPA"],
 "BATNA is the best alternative to a negotiated agreement. Knowing it tells a negotiator when to walk away; ZOPA is the zone where both sides' limits overlap."),
("A new project team argues over roles and how to approach the work. Which Tuckman stage is it in?",
 "Storming", ["Forming", "Norming", "Performing"],
 "Storming is when members push back on roles and methods. Forming is the polite start, and norming comes once roles and rules are agreed."),
("Two coworkers disagree about the order in which to complete a project's tasks. This is:",
 "Task conflict", ["Relationship conflict", "Interorganizational conflict", "Role conflict"],
 "Task conflict concerns the work itself, such as goals, methods or sequence. Relationship conflict is about personal friction."),
("Two team leads are locked in a heated dispute over shared staff, and both ask HR to decide. What should HR do first?",
 "Meet jointly to explore needs", ["Rule for the more senior lead", "Let their teams vote on it", "Tell them to settle it alone"],
 "Bringing both leads together to surface their interests often finds a schedule both can use. Ruling for seniority ignores the merits."),
("An HR specialist needs IT's help on a project but has no contacts there. Best first step?",
 "Learn the IT lead's priorities", ["Send IT a project charter", "Ask the CIO to name a contact", "Log a ticket in the IT queue"],
 "Relationship building starts by understanding what the other group needs, which makes later requests easier. Going over the lead's head or through a queue first skips that step."),
("Two employees snipe over who gets a window desk. Work is unaffected, and the team moves offices next week. Best response?",
 "Let the move settle it", ["Start formal mediation", "Issue written warnings", "Assign desks by seniority"],
 "When an issue is minor and will resolve itself soon, avoiding is the efficient choice. Formal mediation or warnings would escalate a trivial dispute."),
("A manager tells his HR partner that HR \"never helps.\" What should the HR partner do first?",
 "Ask for specific examples", ["Defend HR's record on service", "Report him to his director", "Ignore the remark"],
 "Specific examples show whether there is a real service gap and give a basis for fixing it. Defending HR first shuts the conversation down."),
("A top candidate asks for more than the salary range allows. The range is firm. Best response?",
 "Negotiate other offer terms", ["Exceed the range anyway", "Withdraw the offer entirely", "Promise a raise next month"],
 "Start date, relocation help, flexibility or a sign-on bonus where policy allows can close the gap without breaking the range. A promise HR cannot keep damages trust."),
("A cross-functional team keeps missing deadlines because no one owns the tasks. What should HR recommend?",
 "Assign an owner to each task", ["Add several more team members", "Hold longer and more frequent meetings", "Replace the team leader with a peer"],
 "Clear ownership and accountability fix the root cause. Adding people to a team with unclear roles usually makes coordination worse."),
("A peer department asks to move a joint training date. HR has no conflict with either date. Best response?",
 "Agree to the new date", ["Insist on the original date", "Escalate to both directors", "Cancel the joint session"],
 "When the issue matters more to the other party and costs HR nothing, accommodating builds goodwill. Holding firm here would only create friction."),
("In the basic communication model, the receiver's response back to the sender is:",
 "Feedback", ["Encoding", "Noise", "Channel"],
 "Feedback closes the loop and shows whether the message was understood. Noise is anything that distorts the message, and the channel is the medium."),
("Restating a speaker's point in your own words to confirm you understood it is:",
 "Paraphrasing", ["Probing", "Mirroring body language", "Closed questioning"],
 "Paraphrasing checks understanding and shows attention. Probing asks for more detail rather than confirming what was said."),
("Which question type best draws out an employee's own reasons in a stay interview?",
 "Open-ended", ["Closed", "Leading", "Hypothetical"],
 "Open-ended questions such as \"What keeps you here?\" invite detail. Closed questions get yes or no, and leading questions push a preferred answer."),
("A layoff will affect 40 people in one unit. How should HR deliver the news to them?",
 "In face-to-face meetings", ["By company-wide email to the unit", "Through a notice on the board", "By recorded video"],
 "Sensitive news needs a rich channel where people can ask questions and get support. Written or recorded notices feel impersonal and leave questions unanswered."),
("A routine reminder of the benefits enrollment deadline must reach all 3,000 staff. Best channel?",
 "Email and intranet post", ["One-on-one meetings", "A town hall meeting only", "Calls from each manager"],
 "Routine, factual messages to a large group suit lean, written channels people can refer back to. Meetings with every employee would waste time."),
("An executive's announcement of a new policy confused frontline staff with jargon. What should HR do?",
 "Issue a plain-language summary", ["Repeat the announcement", "Wait for questions to arrive", "Train staff on the jargon"],
 "Translating technical language for the audience is a core communication skill. Repeating the same words will not fix the confusion."),
("During a feedback meeting, an employee crosses his arms and goes silent. What should the HR partner do?",
 "Pause and ask for his view", ["Speed up to finish quickly", "End the meeting at once", "Continue without comment"],
 "Nonverbal cues suggest disagreement or discomfort. Checking in invites the concern into the open before the meeting moves on."),
("Which of these is an element of nonverbal communication?",
 "Proximity", ["Word choice", "Sentence length", "Message timing"],
 "Nonverbal elements include eye contact, body language, gestures and proximity, the physical distance between people. Word choice is verbal."),
("A manager asks how to give critical feedback to a high performer. Best advice?",
 "Name the behavior and its impact", ["Save it for the annual review meeting", "Raise it in the weekly team meeting", "Send it only in writing"],
 "Effective feedback is timely, specific and private, naming the behavior and its effect. Waiting for the annual review lets the problem continue."),
("Rumors of a merger are spreading, but leaders cannot disclose details yet. What should HR advise?",
 "Share what can be said, and when", ["Deny that any merger talks are happening", "Say nothing at all until the deal closes", "Release the confidential deal terms"],
 "Acknowledging what is known and when more will come builds trust while protecting confidential terms. Denial damages credibility once the news breaks."),
("A focus group facilitator's main job is to:",
 "Draw out every member's view", ["Defend company decisions", "Hold a vote on solutions", "Record only the majority's views"],
 "Facilitators guide discussion so all members contribute, including dissenting voices. Defending decisions discourages honest input."),
("An anonymous survey comment alleges that a manager is stealing. What should HR do?",
 "Check records for supporting facts", ["Ignore it because it is anonymous", "Confront the manager publicly", "Trace who wrote the comment"],
 "An anonymous tip still deserves a discreet look at the evidence. Tracing the author breaks the survey's promise and chills future reporting."),
("A remote employee says she misses key news that coworkers pass along in hallway talk. Best fix?",
 "Post updates in a shared channel", ["Require her to come to the office", "Ban hallway conversations", "Tell her to ask coworkers for the news"],
 "A written channel everyone can see gives remote and on-site staff the same information. Banning informal talk is neither practical nor needed."),
("Which behavior is a sign of active listening?",
 "Asking clarifying questions", ["Planning your reply", "Finishing the speaker's sentences", "Checking messages while the speaker talks"],
 "Active listening means full attention and checking understanding through questions and paraphrase. Planning a reply while the other person talks splits attention."),
]
